  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure remains over the east coast through Thursday. \nSoutherly flow on the backside of the departing high will bring \nwell above normal temperatures (highs in the 70s, lows in \n40s/50s), potentially challenging record high temperatures on \nThursday (see climate section below).\n\nA low pressure system located well to the north near the Georgian\nBay will bring a cold front across the area Thursday night. \nWhile moisture and lift is still lacking overall, precipitation\nhave increased to around 30-60%. Some hi-res model guidance\nindicate a decent chance at a brief, moderate rainfall with some\nof the heavier rain showers, mainly near Lake Erie where there\ncould be slightly high low-lelel moisture/convergence. \n\nHigh pressure builds back in Friday through Sunday, ushering \n\"cooler\" but still above normal temperatures with highs in the \n60s and lows in the 40s/50s. Some of the typically cooler spots\nof Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania could get down \ninto the mid 30s Friday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs high pressure departs to the east, broad isentropic lift\nbrings rain chances back Sunday night into early Monday. Latest\nmodel guidance has trended toward lower PoPs and a drier day \nMonday and even into Monday night as precip is mainly tied to a\nwarm front that lifts northeastward across the area Sunday\nnight. Any rain that does occur will be light and likely less \nthan 0.1\".\n\nA low pressure system and upper-level low approach the area from\nthe west, with rain chances on the rise midweek. This system has\na better chance to bring appreciable rainfall.\n\nTemperatures will likely remain above normal into next week with\nhighs in the upper 60s and lows in the 40s/50s.",
